      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2002 Elsevier Science B.V.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "No detailed studies exist of coronary artery bypass graft flow during atrial fibrillation. We examined the effects on bypass graft flow of atrial fibrillation following coronary artery bypass grafting."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Immediately after surgical revisualization, atrial fibrillation was induced in 18 patients by high frequency atrial pacing. Hemodynamic variables were measured in sinus rhythm and atrial fibrillation. The graft flow in pedicled left internal thoracic artery grafts and in saphenous vein grafts was also measured using transit-time flowmetry."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Left internal thoracic artery graft flow had a greater diastolic component than saphenous vein graft flow, as shown by the percent diastolic time-flow integral (86 +/- 10% in the left thoracic artery and 62 +/- 12% in the saphenous vein, P < 0.0001). The induced atrial fibrillation caused significant deterioration in hemodynamics: heart rate and central venous pressure increased, and mean arterial pressure and cardiac index decreased (all P < 0.0025). In left internal thoracic artery grafts (n = 18) and also in saphenous vein grafts (n = 20), graft flow decreased significantly with atrial fibrillation (44.3 +/- 26.2 to 26.2 +/- 20.7 ml/min in the left internal thoracic artery, P = 0.0003; 39.7 +/- 15.6 to 33.3 +/- 14.3 ml/min in the saphenous vein, P = 0.001). The reduction in graft flow due to atrial fibrillation was much larger in left internal thoracic artery grafts than in saphenous vein grafts (P = 0.0008)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Direct measurement of coronary artery bypass graft flow shows that atrial fibrillation after surgery significantly reduces graft flow. The effect is much larger in left internal thoracic artery grafts with their strong diastolic component than in saphenous vein grafts."
          }
        ]
      },
